369-/// Every loop in `block`, found from its backward jumps.
370-///
371-/// A loop is the backward `JUMP` the compiler writes at the end of every loop
372-/// body: it targets the `STORE` that binds the loop variable for `for each` and
373-/// `repeat`, and the first instruction of the condition for a `while`. No other
374-/// jump in the instruction set goes backwards, so that jump *is* the loop and
375-/// nothing in the file has to say where one begins.
376-///
377-/// A `while` is told from the other three by what precedes its `top`: a
378-/// `GET_ITER`, or a `GET_RANGE` whose operand count says whether it is a
379-/// `repeat` count or a pair of bounds. A `while` has neither, because its `top`
380-/// is its condition.
381-///
382-/// `exit` is where the loop is left. A sequence loop runs out at the instruction
383-/// after its backward jump. A `while` leaves through its own `JUMP_IF_FALSE`, so
384-/// that is the instruction sought: the one in the loop's body that jumps past the
385-/// backward jump.
