**Gates** (re-run on the tree as it stands):

| Gate | Result |
|---|---|
| `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | pass, no diff |
| `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | pass, zero warnings |
| `cargo test --all-targets` | **821 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored** |
| `cargo test --doc` | 1 passed |
| `./rbops/verify.sh phase-025` | **not run — `rbops/` is absent from this checkout**; substitute run by hand: `rb test` 332/332, all 6 `examples/*.rb` and both `modules/*.rb` exit 0, `tests/loop_bounds_test.rs` 22, `tests/loop_control_test.rs` 61, `tests/bytecode_vm_test.rs` 60, `tests/differential_test.rs` 81 |

**What I found and fixed.** The tree arrived green, so I swept the two engines against each other across loop forms × exit paths × lowered caps. That turned up a disagreement the corpus structurally cannot see — the corpus runs at the published cap of a million and no program can lower it:

- **BLOCKER** — a `while` of *exactly* N turns was refused at a cap of N on the bytecode VM. At the published cap this needs no harness: `while n < 1000000` printed `1000000` and exited 0 under `rb run`, and exited 1 with `Maximum of 1000000 iterations reached` under `rb vm`. Round 6's own fix (to make a `skip` cost a turn) had moved the charge to the backward `JUMP` — the wrong side of the condition — so the off-by-one it removed came straight back. Its test could not see it: every case in it ran until the cap stopped it.
- **MAJOR** — a `while` that ended on its condition never dropped its loop entry, so the next turn of an *enclosing* loop re-charged it. Two inner loops of two turns each cost four; a nested `while` hit a cap four times its own turn count.

Both are one correction: a turn is charged where it **begins** (`JUMP_IF_FALSE` on truthy, via a new `condition_site`) and a loop gives its entry back where it **ends** (falsy `JUMP_IF_FALSE` gives it back, as a sequence loop does by running out of values). `jump` and `turn_over_to` now charge nothing — a `skip` lands on the instruction that begins the next turn, and that charges it.

**Verification.** Two tests, both watched failing before the fix; the nesting case was watched failing *again* with only the entry-dropping half restored, so neither half is carried by the other. Each asks the boundary from both sides: a cap of exactly the turns taken runs and finishes, one turn short is the cap that stops it, naming itself, on both engines — across `while`, `while`+`skip`, `repeat`, `for each`, `while`-in-`while`, and both jumps.

I also corrected REPORT.md's record rather than leaving it: round 6's gates section asserted "a cap of N is N turns on both engines", which was false. It is now marked wrong, struck through, and superseded in *Invariants touched* and *Known gaps*.

`phases/phase-025/FINDINGS.md` gains §17 with both programs and the reasoning.

